<SddAmplifyNfr keywords="nfr, amplify, budgets, latency, throughput, scale, availability, consistency, bottlenecks, degradation, sdd-amplify-nfr" type="directive" ver="2.0">
  <Mission>
    Turn performance and reliability signals into ordinary atomic requirements: latency /
    throughput / scale / availability / consistency as numbers, plus observable overload
    behaviour. Loaded lazily by the discovery interview's triage when the scope carries load,
    growth, or availability expectations; inherits the session core — not repeated here.

    Input — the scope spec draft + interview context (usage profile signals). Output — flat
    requirement entries in the canonical Requirements section + follow-up items on the discovery
    interview map. Out of scope: benchmark methodology and load-test tooling — detailed material
    belongs in Architecture or one referenced supporting section only when genuinely needed.
  </Mission>

  <BeliefState deps="AX_OPERATOR_LANGUAGE, AX_OPERATOR_SAFEGUARD, AX_NO_PROCESS_NARRATION">
    {{> "axiom/process/ax-amplify-defaults-first"}}

    {{> "axiom/spec/ax-decision-log-required"}}

    {{> "axiom/perf/ax-budgets-are-numbers"}}

    {{> "axiom/perf/ax-latency-stacking"}}

    {{> "axiom/perf/ax-backpressure-is-law"}}
  </BeliefState>

  <HaltConditions>
    | 🛑 ID | Trigger |
    |---|---|
    | `H_NO_USAGE_SIGNAL` | Neither the spec draft nor the operator gives any scale / load signal to budget against — return the question to the interview |
    | `H_OPERATOR_REJECTS_DEFAULTS` | Operator declines the whole defaults package and skips deep-dive — leave the spec untouched |
  </HaltConditions>

  <ExecutionPlan>
    <Step id="STEP_0_PROPOSE_DEFAULTS">
      <Goal>Assemble the numeric budget package for this product class.</Goal>
      <Action>
        Extract the usage profile from the spec draft (who calls what, how often, how much data).
        Per `AX_AMPLIFY_DEFAULTS_FIRST`, draft one observable outcome per relevant dimension — latency,
        throughput, scale + growth horizon, availability, consistency — with numbers live products
        of this class actually hold (per `AX_BUDGETS_ARE_NUMBERS`; short targeted research when
        class practice is uncertain). Name the expected first bottleneck and the degradation
        posture under overload (shed / queue / reject — mind `AX_BACKPRESSURE_IS_LAW`,
        `AX_LATENCY_STACKING`). A dimension with no design consequence is not an entry. No usage
        signal at all → `H_NO_USAGE_SIGNAL`.
      </Action>
    </Step>
    <Step id="STEP_1_AGREE">
      <Goal>One accept-or-edit gate; deep-dive only on explicit pick.</Goal>
      <Action>
        Present the candidate outcomes as ONE compact reply per `AX_AMPLIFY_DEFAULTS_FIRST` (AskUserQuestion when
        available, else `QUESTION_FORMAT`); each dimension accept / edit / deep-dive. Deep-dive
        topic map: load profile → latency budget → scale horizon → availability + error budget →
        consistency → bottlenecks → degradation. A dimension the operator consciously skips gets an
        explicit «не бюджетируем, потому что …» row. STOP.
      </Action>
    </Step>
    <Step id="STEP_2_APPLY">
      <Goal>Write the agreed outcomes as current-format requirements.</Goal>
      <Action>
        Use the Requirements section's embedded grammar; do not preload a separate format.
        Continue the spec's next unused sequential `<ACR>-REQ-<N>` IDs. Write one ordinary flat
        entry per accepted observable outcome, with modality class, behaviour sentence, and short
        `Constraint`, `Verification`, and `Trace` lines; quantified budgets stay numeric. Never
        create `### Non-Functional Constraints`, another topic heading, or a table inside
        Requirements.

        If the existing Requirements section is legacy split/topic format, read it as input and
        migrate the whole section to flat entries in this already-approved edit; preserve every
        still-valid outcome and do not leave a mixed representation or ask for a second approval.
        A benchmark/load-profile table belongs in Architecture by default, or in one explicitly
        named supporting section outside Requirements only when the detail is genuinely needed;
        link it from `Trace`. Non-default picks and notable accepted defaults → Decision Log per
        `AX_DECISION_LOG_REQUIRED`. Budgets still needing reality validation → discovery interview
        map, not vague requirements. Return control to the interview.
      </Action>
    </Step>
  </ExecutionPlan>

  <ChatOutput>
    {{> "contract/process/question-format"}}

    Service line — dim, internal, never narrated (`AX_NO_PROCESS_NARRATION`):
    `📐 AMP_NFR · dims=<n> · accepted=<n> · edited=<n> · dive=<n>`.
  </ChatOutput>
</SddAmplifyNfr>
